  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry conditions continue through mid week, then \ncloudy & rainy towards the end of the week with an incoming cold\nfront.\n\nThe stretch of benign weather conditions is expected to continue \nthrough the middle of the work week. Surface high pressure centered \nover the Northeast today is going to slide offshore tonight into \nMonday. The ridge then remains nearly stationary over the western \nAtlantic and maintains its influence across the region. The surface \nridge becomes somewhat vertically stacked as a mid-to upper ridge \nbuilds over the Carolinas Tuesday into Wednesday. While return \nsoutherly flow does bring temperatures back to the 70s Tuesday into \nWednesday, conditions are expected to remain dry as widespread \nsubsidence dominates and no appreciable moisture advection into the \nregion occurs until late in the week. Speaking of, a strong upper \ntrough does approach from the OH Valley Wednesday, but is slowed in \nits progress due to a stronger, more stacked ridge to its east. \nScattered showers reach the Alleghenies sometime Wednesday afternoon \nto evening, but most of that activity should be light given the \nlingering dry low-level airmass. \n\nHigh temperatures in the low to mid 70s Tuesday and Wednesday are \ngoing to be well above normal for early November. Daily high \ntemperature records for the first week of November at all of our \nclimate stations are mostly in the low 80s, so not expecting any \nrecord breaking warmth at this time.\n\nSurface high pressure begins to break down around midweek as a \nstrong upper-level trough forms over the Great Lakes before pushing \neastward. Cloud cover likely begins to increase throughout the day \nWednesday as the stemming cold front approaches sometime \nbetween Thursday and Friday. This will bring the first chance \nfor widespread beneficial rain throughout the region for the \nfirst time in over two weeks. Total QPF amounts remain uncertain\nat this time, but could accumulate in amounts averaging 0.5-1.5\ninches throughout the region as the front pushes through. \n\nAdditional rain is possible next Monday & into early next week as a \nsecond cold front moves in from the west. Temperatures will cool to \nmore seasonable conditions as these fronts move through, with highs \nin the mid-60s and lows in the 40s at the start of next week. \nConditions become drier towards the middle of next week as the \ntrough transitions offshore.",
